<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shudra">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shudra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shudra is the fourth of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>, the classes of traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society described in Vedic and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts, below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishyas</a>. Shudras were assigned the duty of serving the other three classes and were excluded from Vedic study and ritual. Several ancient dynasties, including the Nandas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, were said in hostile sources to be of Shudra origin.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indian and Greek sources agree that the first Nanda was of humble origin: the Puranas call him the son of a <b>Shudra</b> woman, and the Roman historian Quintus Curtius Rufus reports that the founder of the dynasty was a barber.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The law books describe four varnas, or classes: Brahmins, priests and teachers; Kshatriyas, rulers and warriors; Vaishyas, merchants and farmers; and <b>Shudras</b>, labourers and servants.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Early on the tribal chief (rajan) and nobility (rajanya) stood at the top, the common people (viś) in the middle and the dasa and dasyu at the bottom; the Rigveda does not yet link the words Brahmana and Kshatriya to varna, and Vaishya and <b>Shudra</b> are absent.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The varna scheme of the ancient texts placed the Brahmins, the priests, at the head of four orders, above the Kshatriyas (rulers and warriors), the Vaishyas (traders) and the <b>Shudras</b> (labourers), with Dalits and Adivasis outside.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brahmanical law books of this age, the Dharmashastras such as the Manusmriti, ranked people in four varnas fixed by birth: Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas and <b>Shudras</b>, with groups such as the Chandalas placed outside them.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
